On Jul 14, 5:24 pm, "Joseph N. Hall" <remid0d...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Jul 14, 2:14 pm, "John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> This has wandered. What I am hoping to get across is that the issues
> associated with farming and other "generate lots of random objects in
> hoping of getting some good ones" strategies are subtle and sometimes
> counterintuitive. In another community of mine, we call this the "XY
> problem." The "XY problem" is someone asking how to do X because he
> actually wants to do Y, but X is the wrong way to do it. I strongly
> believe that what most people view as solution X, "stop pudding
> farming," is really a misguided attempt at solving a different problem
> Y. Unfortunately, problem Y isn't agreed upon or perhaps even known,
> and whatever the solution to it is, it isn't X.

I happen to have a problem Y that I would like fixed: to me, infinite
generation feels inelegant. One resource, a pudding, should not be
able to generate an infinite number of items. I have no problem with
generating a very, very large number of items, and with an extinction
limit of 120 parent puddings (of each kind!), one ought to be able to
farm them a very long time with a simple change: perhaps when they
split the offspring has slight chance of losing a point or two of
maxHP. Carefully managed, 120 parent black puddings and 120 parent
brown puddings ought to still generate a LOT of puddings. I don't
suppose monsters benefit from the "increase max HP" effect of healing
potions? If they do, then assuming the puddings are allowing you to
get more potions than they are losing HP, you could still farm them
quasi-indefinitely until by bad luck (or the law of averages) you get
no death-drops for a long time, and can't raise their max HP anymore.
I would recommend a similar implementation for gremlins, and any other
monsters that split that I've forgotten about. I've heard (though I've
not verified) that Trolls ressurrect with new equipment, which makes
very little sense and shhuld probably go too. I should also think that
monsters in the Quest should respect extinction, if there are going to
be extinction limits at all.

Thus, pudding farmers could still farm their puddings (though they'd
need to pay attention, and occasionally go get another one) and the
generation would no longer be truly infinite, but the chance of loss
of maxHP could be made low enough to generate quite enough puddings
for all but the most persistent 'hackers.



